  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1142 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nCloudy conditions will continue Saturday, as the southern branch \nof the upper jet brings mid and high level Pacific moisture into \nthe Midsouth. A weak cold front passed through the region Friday \nafternoon, aided by convective outflow. Since then, north/northeast\nwinds have prevailed and will continue to do so Saturday.\n\nWhile more moderate temperatures are expected, humidity will \nlinger until a stronger cold frontal passage late Sunday afternoon\ninto Sunday evening. This reinforcing cold front will coincide \nwith a northern branch trough passage through the upper Midwest on\nSunday. A modified continental polar airmass will bring \nnoticeably lower humidity, and cooler overnight lows. Tuesday \nmorning will see the surface pressure ridge settle into the \nMidsouth, setting up prime radiational cooling conditions. Lows \nwill mainly range from the upper 40s to lower 50s. Some river \nvalleys of West Tennessee could see lows in the mid 40s.\n\nFor the mid to late week periods, a large upper level ridge over\nthe Southwest U.S. will expand eastward, displacing weak\nnorthwest flow over the Midsouth. The net result will be daytime\nhighs warming well into the 80s (7 to 10 degrees above normal)\nwith plenty of sunshine.\n\nBy next weekend, medium range guidance consensus begins to fade\nwith respect to northern branch energy over the western CONUS.\nCloser to home, there is a signal in the GFS and ECMWF data for a\nweak easterly tropical wave along the Gulf coast, extending into\nthe lower MS River Valley. Consensus aside, confidence is limited\nregarding this system bringing consequential rainfall to the\nMidsouth next weekend.",
